The Indian Act of Canada: A History of Control
What Was the Indian Act?
The Indian Act is a major Canadian federal law that started in 1876. Its main goal was to try
and make First Nation and Indigenous people live and act like the rest of Canadian society. This
is called assimilation.
● Assimilation means making one group of people become exactly like a larger, more
powerful group. In this case, the government wanted First Nation people to give up their
languages, cultures, and traditions and live like European-Canadians.
The Act gave the Canadian government almost total control over the lives of First Nation and
Indigenous people, including their land, health, and education.
The Problem: The Act was supposed to help integrate people, but it actually did the opposite.
By controlling everything, it kept First Nation people separated and isolated from the rest of
Canadian society.

How the Act Started
In 1867, when Canada became a country (Confederation), the new federal government was put
in charge of most Aboriginal people (the original people of North America) and their reserved
lands.
Before the Indian Act, there were many smaller laws controlling how Aboriginal people lived
near white settlers. By 1876, the government decided to gather all these laws into one big one:
the Indian Act.
Defining "Indian"
The Act had a specific way of deciding who was legally considered an "Indian." This person
was called a Status Indian. The government completely controlled this definition.
● The Act only applied to Status Indians who belonged to a recognized Band (a
community) with a reserve or treaty.
● Other Aboriginal groups like the Inuit (in the Arctic) and the Métis (people of mixed
European and Aboriginal heritage) were not covered by the Act, but they also faced
struggles without the benefits the Act provided.

Strict Rules and Lost Rights
The Indian Act controlled nearly everything a Status Indian could or couldn't do.

