Loss of Status
If an Indian wanted to become a full Canadian citizen, they had to be enfranchised. This meant
meeting certain white standards (like becoming a lawyer or a farmer) and, most importantly,
giving up their Status Indian rights forever. This was the government's way of forcing
assimilation.

Changes Since 1951
After World War II (1939-1945), people in Canada became more aware of the unfairness of the
Act. They saw that First Nation soldiers had fought bravely, but many people were living in
poverty because of the Act's rules.
In 1951, Parliament finally made major changes to the law:
● It gave some self-government to First Nation communities (Bands).
● The bans on traditional ceremonies (like the Sun Dance) were removed.
● The strict rules on drinking alcohol and leaving the reserve were removed.

What is Self-Government?
● Self-government means that a community or nation has the power to make decisions
for itself, manage its own land, laws, and finances, and choose its own leaders, without
another government having complete control over them.
More Recent Changes:
● By 1962, Status Indians finally gained the right to vote in federal and provincial
elections.
● In 1985, a big change made it so women who married a non-Indigenous person no
longer automatically lost their Status.
● In the 1980s and 90s, the government gave Bands more power to decide who could be
a member of their community and live on the reserve.

The Act's Legacy Today
Despite these changes, the Indian Act is still active and remains one of the most controlling
laws in Canada.
● It continues to limit self-government for First Nation communities.
● It has often failed to protect First Nation land and has made economic development
on reserves very difficult.
Most First Nation people want to see the Indian Act abolished because it is a tool of control.
However, they also know that the Act is the only law that officially recognizes their special rights
and land. The goal now is to get rid of the control, but keep the benefits and gain true
self-government.




